## Boolean EntryStore settings (entrystore.* and logging.http.enabled) accept, case-insensitively:
## true/on/yes/1 to enable and false/off/no/0 to disable; surrounding whitespace is ignored.
## An unrecognised value fails startup for settings read by the REST layer (e.g. entrystore.cors,
## entrystore.auth.recaptcha, entrystore.backup.scheduler, entrystore.traversal.<profile>.repository-scope,
## logging.http.enabled); settings read by the repository core (e.g. entrystore.solr,
## entrystore.data.quota, entrystore.harvester.oai) log a warning and use their default.
## Leave a setting out rather than blank: a blank value fails startup for some settings.
## Spring Boot and third-party settings (spring.*, server.*, management.*, springdoc.*) must use
## true/false: some of them treat off, no or 0 as enabled.

## Base URI how it is seen from external clients.
## When running behind a reverse proxy, configure the proxy to send
## X-Forwarded-Proto, X-Forwarded-Host, X-Forwarded-Port, and X-Forwarded-Prefix headers.
## Spring Boot's ForwardedHeaderFilter (server.forward-headers-strategy=framework
## in application.yaml) processes these headers for request URL resolution.
## SECURITY: Spring does NOT validate which client sent the X-Forwarded-* headers,
## so the reverse proxy MUST strip them from untrusted (client-originating) requests
## before forwarding. Otherwise a client can spoof X-Forwarded-Proto/Host/Port/Prefix
## to influence baseURL resolution and potentially poison shared caches.
entrystore.baseurl.folder=http://entrystore.org/store/

## Repository store: memory | native | http | sparql
entrystore.repository.store.type=native
## If store type is native these properties must be specified
## If store type is memory a path can be specified for persisting the repository
entrystore.repository.store.path=file:///srv/entrystore/store/
entrystore.repository.store.indexes=cspo,posc
## if type=http: entrystore.repository.store.url=URL of Sesame HTTP repository
## if type=sparql: entrystore.repository.store.endpoint-query=Query URL of SPARQL repository
## if type=sparql: entrystore.repository.store.endpoint-update=Update URL of SPARQL repository

## Populate an empty repository with test data (from TestSuite)
# entrystore.repository.store.init-with-test-data=on|off (default: off)

## Data folder
entrystore.data.folder=/srv/entrystore/files/

## Backup
# entrystore.backup.scheduler=on|off (default: off)
# entrystore.backup.folder=/srv/entrystore/backup/
# entrystore.backup.timeregexp=time regular expression as for cron jobs
# entrystore.backup.gzip=on|off (default: off)
# entrystore.backup.maintenance=on|off (default: off)
# entrystore.backup.maintenance.upper-limit=maximum amount of stored backups
# entrystore.backup.maintenance.lower-limit=minimum amount of stored backups
# entrystore.backup.maintenance.expires-after-days=maximum days a backup is kept

## Cross-origin resource sharing (CORS)
# entrystore.cors=on|off (default: off)
# entrystore.cors.origins=*.domain1.com,domain2.*
# entrystore.cors.headers=X-Custom-Header
# entrystore.cors.max-age=7200

## Cross-site request forgery (CSRF) protection.
## When enabled, cookie-authenticated mutating requests (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE) must echo the
## XSRF-TOKEN cookie value in the X-XSRF-TOKEN request header. When disabled, no token is
## required and no XSRF-TOKEN cookie is issued. SECURITY: the default is off because common
## EntryStore clients do not send the token header yet (ENTRYSTORE-1008); enable this once all
## cookie-authenticated clients of the installation send X-XSRF-TOKEN, since without it cookie
## sessions are open to CSRF attacks.
# entrystore.csrf.enabled=true|false (default: false)
## Name of the CSRF token cookie.
# entrystore.csrf.cookie-name=XSRF-TOKEN

## HTTP Server response header.
## Default: EntryStore/<version-from-VERSION.txt>.
## A non-blank string here wins over the auto-computed default. Blank or unset falls through.
# entrystore.http.header.server=Acme Portal

## Or control how much of the auto-computed default's version is exposed.
## Values: full (default; e.g. EntryStore/6.0-SNAPSHOT), minor (EntryStore/6.0),
## major (EntryStore/6), none (bare EntryStore). The minor and major modes strip the
## Maven qualifier (e.g. -SNAPSHOT, +build.x). Ignored when the explicit override above
## is set to a non-blank value.
# entrystore.http.header.server.version-precision=major

## JSONP support (default: true)
## When on, a GET with a ?callback= parameter has its JSON response body wrapped as
## callback(<json>) with Content-Type application/javascript. Legacy technique — CORS
## (see entrystore.cors above) is the preferred mechanism and JSONP can be disabled here.
# entrystore.jsonp=true

## SMTP settings, required for e.g. sign-up
## Transport security is `entrystore.smtp.security`. This file documented it as `entrystore.smtp.ssl`,
## a key the code never read, up to and including 5.6.2 — deployments configured from one of those
## copies were therefore sending plaintext. 5.7.0 through 5.8.0 documented the canonical key
## correctly. As of 6.1 both spellings are honoured: `entrystore.smtp.security` is read whenever it
## resolves, and `entrystore.smtp.ssl` only as a fallback. Setting both is allowed; if they disagree,
## `security` applies and the deprecated key is reported at WARN. Values are matched on letters and
## digits only, case-insensitively, so `STARTTLS`, `start-tls` and the `START_TLS` form an environment
## variable naturally carries all resolve alike.
## Neither key was validated before 6.1 — `ssl` was never read, and `security` was compared with
## equalsIgnoreCase — so a value outside starttls|ssl|off silently meant plaintext. Such a value is now
## reported at ERROR and NO MAIL IS SENT while it stays set: sending in the clear under a configuration
## meant to be encrypted is not a safe default. Startup is never aborted over it — a mail
## misconfiguration must not take the REST API down. A configured host that ends up on plaintext
## without `security` being set at all is warned about at startup, so a missing setting is loud rather
## than silent; setting `entrystore.smtp.security=off` declares plaintext and silences that warning.
## `entrystore.smtp.ssl` is deprecated and will be removed in a future major version.
##
## UPGRADE NOTE: because `entrystore.smtp.ssl` is now honoured and an unresolvable value now refuses to
## send, transport security is enforced rather than downgraded, and a deployment carrying a stale value
## in either key will see sends start FAILING rather than continuing insecurely:
##   - `entrystore.smtp.ssl=ssl` with the `entrystore.smtp.port=587` this file documented alongside it
##     means implicit TLS on a submission port, so every send fails.
##   - `entrystore.smtp.ssl=starttls` against a relay that does not advertise STARTTLS now throws
##     instead of silently downgrading.
##   - a value neither key can resolve — `security=tls`, `=none`, `=true` — refuses to send at all.
## Sign-up, password reset, POST /message and the password-change confirmations stop working in each of
## those cases. Verify your MTA supports the implied mode before upgrading, and declare a deliberately
## plaintext relay explicitly with `entrystore.smtp.security=off`.
# entrystore.smtp.host=smtp.host.tld
# entrystore.smtp.port=587 (default: 25)
# entrystore.smtp.security=starttls|ssl|off (default: off)
## Both credential keys must be set. If only one is, that is reported at ERROR and SMTP
## authentication is disabled — the session stays anonymous, as before 6.1 — rather than failing
## startup.
# entrystore.smtp.username=smtp-user
# entrystore.smtp.password=smtp-password
## Envelope addresses. These supersede entrystore.auth.email.from|bcc, which are still read as a
## fallback and log a deprecation warning at startup when used. A value that is not a valid address is
## reported at ERROR at startup, naming the key. bcc and reply-to may each be a comma-separated list —
## legal for Reply-To under RFC 5322 and accepted here since 2019 — while from must be exactly one
## mailbox. The recipient and the Reply-To of POST /message are caller-supplied rather than configured
## here, and must each be exactly one mailbox: no list, no RFC822 group address and no CR or LF.
# entrystore.smtp.email.from=signup@yourdomain.tld
# entrystore.smtp.email.bcc=info+signup@yourdomain.tld
# entrystore.smtp.email.reply-to=support@yourdomain.tld
## Socket timeouts and TLS server-identity checking, hardcoded before 6.1. The defaults below are the
## values that were previously compiled in; lower them only if your MTA is reliably fast.
## Disabling check-server-identity makes TLS vulnerable to an active man-in-the-middle — it exists for
## test MTAs with self-signed certificates, not for production.
# entrystore.smtp.connection-timeout=5s
# entrystore.smtp.read-timeout=5s
# entrystore.smtp.write-timeout=5s
# entrystore.smtp.check-server-identity=true
## TOTAL number of send attempts before giving up, not the number of retries: 3 means two retries and
## 1 disables retrying. Must be between 1 and 10. Attempts are immediate (no backoff), so a single send
## can occupy its thread for this many times the whole SMTP exchange. Note that read-timeout above
## bounds each individual read, not the exchange, and one exchange makes seven or so blocking reads
## (greeting, EHLO, MAIL FROM, RCPT TO, DATA, end-of-data, QUIT) — plus DNS resolution, which no
## timeout covers. Which thread depends on the path: sign-up, POST /message, the /auth/pwreset
## confirmation and a user password change all send on the request thread, while password-reset mail
## runs on the passwordResetExecutor (2 threads, queue of 100, abort policy), so raising this value
## there fills that queue until dispatches are rejected rather than slowing individual requests.
## A permanent failure is not retried at all — a message that will not parse, an address the server
## rejected, or an authentication failure the server did not report with a 4xx code. A message the
## transport reports as partially delivered is never re-sent, so that path cannot produce a duplicate
## password-reset link; a failure that reports no delivered addresses at all, such as a read timeout
## after DATA, is still retried and can. The startup log warns when the timeouts and this value multiply
## out to a long block.
# entrystore.smtp.max-send-attempts=3
##
## Note: the spring.mail.* keys are not used; EntryStore builds its mail sender from the
## entrystore.smtp.* keys above. spring.mail.test-connection currently has no effect, because Spring
## Boot's validator requires a single JavaMailSenderImpl bean and EntryStore declares the bean as the
## JavaMailSender interface. Keep that declared type — narrowing it would make the flag open an SMTP
## connection during startup.

## Rate limiting for POST /message (0 to disable)
# entrystore.message.rate.limit.max=10
# entrystore.message.rate.limit.window=1h

## Rate limiting for POST /auth/signup by client IP
## SECURITY: the client IP comes from the Forwarded (for=) header, else the leftmost
## X-Forwarded-For entry (server.forward-headers-strategy in application.yaml), for every request
## that carries them. The reverse proxy must drop client-supplied Forwarded and X-Forwarded-*
## headers and set X-Forwarded-For to the connecting client's address, not append to it (nginx:
## proxy_set_header Forwarded ""; proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr;). Otherwise this
## and the other per-IP limits are bypassable by spoofing either header. Without a reverse proxy,
## set server.forward-headers-strategy=none.
# entrystore.auth.signup.rate.limit.max=10
# entrystore.auth.signup.rate.limit.window=1h

## Rate limiting for POST /auth/pwreset by client IP (0 to disable)
# entrystore.auth.password-reset.rate.limit.max=5
# entrystore.auth.password-reset.rate.limit.window=1h

## Search input constraints and per-IP rate limiting for /search.
## The endpoint is guest-accessible; these caps protect Solr from expensive or abusive input.
## The rate limit is disabled by default (max=0). It is keyed by client IP only (Forwarded for=, else
## the leftmost X-Forwarded-For entry; see signup rate limiting) and applies to authenticated users too,
## so clients sharing an IP (pipelines, EntryScape API, users behind NAT) share one budget.
# entrystore.solr.search.rate.limit.max=0
# entrystore.solr.search.rate.limit.window=1m
## The max-length and max-count caps below reject longer input with 400. 0 means unlimited; a negative
## value fails startup. All are unlimited by default, as in 5.x: entrystore.js and EntryScape OR up to
## 20 resource URIs into one query, which is about 2 KB.
# entrystore.solr.search.query.max-length=0
# entrystore.solr.search.sort.max-length=0
# entrystore.solr.search.filter-query.max-length=0
# entrystore.solr.search.filter-query.max-count=0
# entrystore.solr.search.facet-fields.max-length=0
# entrystore.solr.search.facet-fields.max-count=0

## Generic settings for user-initiated sign-up and password reset
## Deprecated: use entrystore.smtp.email.from / .bcc instead. Still honoured as a fallback when the
## entrystore.smtp.email.* equivalent is unset, with a deprecation warning logged at startup.
# entrystore.auth.email.from=signup@yourdomain.tld
# entrystore.auth.email.bcc=info+signup@yourdomain.tld
## With reCAPTCHA on, both keys are required; startup fails if either is missing.
# entrystore.auth.recaptcha=on (default: off)
# entrystore.auth.recaptcha.public-key=your-public-recaptcha-key
# entrystore.auth.recaptcha.private-key=your-private-recaptcha-key

# How long a login lasts: the idle timeout with refresh-expiration-on-access, otherwise a fixed lifetime from login.
# Seconds unless a unit is given (e.g. 1d), at least one second. A form login's auth_maxage can only shorten it.
# (default: 86400)
# entrystore.auth.cookie.max-age=86400
# With on, every request extends the login by max-age, so active users stay logged in; the auth_token cookie then
# lives 365 days and a stolen cookie stays valid while it is used. With off, a login ends max-age after it was
# made and the cookie lives max-age. (default: on)
# entrystore.auth.cookie.refresh-expiration-on-access=on|off

# Path of the auth_token cookie; "auto" uses the path of entrystore.baseurl.folder. (default: auto)
# entrystore.auth.cookie.path=auto
# Whether an unknown or expired auth_token is answered with 401; with off the request is served
# as guest. The cookie is expired in both cases. (default: on)
# entrystore.auth.cookie.invalid-token-error=on|off

## User-initiated sign-up (default: off). When off, /auth/signup and /auth/signup/confirm answer 404.
# entrystore.auth.signup=on|off
# entrystore.auth.signup.email.subject=User sign-up request
# entrystore.auth.signup.email.template=/path/to/template.html
## Whitelist of email-domains that are allowed to perform sign-up
## Any domain is allowed if no whitelist is provided here
## Use the indexed form (.1, .2, ...); a bare "whitelist=domain" value is not supported.
# entrystore.auth.signup.whitelist.1=yourdomain.tld
# entrystore.auth.signup.whitelist.2=ext.yourdomain.tld

## User-initiated password reset (default: off). When off, /auth/pwreset and /auth/pwreset/confirm answer 404.
# entrystore.auth.password-reset=on|off
# entrystore.auth.password-reset.email.subject=Password reset request
# entrystore.auth.password-reset.email.template=/path/to/template.html

## Information about changed password
# entrystore.auth.password-change.email.subject=Your password has been changed
# entrystore.auth.password-change.email.template=/path/to/template.html

## Sign-up / password-reset confirmation behaviour.
## Legacy (default): clicking the emailed confirmation link immediately activates the account or
## applies the password change.
## New (legacy=false): after clicking the link the user must prove they initiated the request before
## anything happens — sign-up requires re-entering the chosen email + password; password reset
## requires the email, then lets the user choose a new password on the confirmation form. The token
## is invalidated after entrystore.auth.confirmation.max-attempts failed attempts.
# entrystore.auth.confirmation.legacy=true
# entrystore.auth.confirmation.max-attempts=3

## Temporary user lockout
entrystore.auth.temp.lockout.max.attempts=0
entrystore.auth.temp.lockout.duration=0ms

## Local password login (default: on)
## on        - every user with a password may log in with it, except those on the blacklist.
## off       - no password login: /auth/cookie and /auth/login answer 404 and HTTP Basic is disabled,
##             even with entrystore.auth.http-basic.enabled=true. SSO logins and existing sessions keep
##             working. Users can still change their password and, if enabled, reset it.
## whitelist - only the usernames listed below may log in with a password, by form login or HTTP
##             Basic. An empty or missing whitelist in this mode denies every password login.
## true/yes/1 and false/no/0 also mean on and off; any other value fails startup.
## Use the indexed form (.1, .2, ...); a bare "whitelist=user" value is not supported.
# entrystore.auth.password=on|off|whitelist
# entrystore.auth.password.whitelist.1=admin
# entrystore.auth.password.whitelist.2=user@yourdomain.tld
# entrystore.auth.password.blacklist.1=blocked@yourdomain.tld
## Require the current password when a user changes their own password (default: true)
# entrystore.auth.password.require-current-password=false

## Rules a new password must meet at sign-up and when set by a user or an admin.
## Defaults: upper case, lower case and a number required, no symbol required, at least 10 characters.
## Each custom rule is a regular expression that must be found in the password; use the indexed form (.1, .2, ...).
# entrystore.auth.password.rule.uppercase=true
# entrystore.auth.password.rule.lowercase=true
# entrystore.auth.password.rule.number=true
# entrystore.auth.password.rule.symbol=false
# entrystore.auth.password.rule.min-length=10
# entrystore.auth.password.rule.custom.1=^\\S+$

## Additional base URLs accepted as a post-login redirect target. The repository base URL
## is always permitted; anything else must be listed here or the redirect is dropped
## (open-redirect protection).
## Use the indexed form (.1, .2, ...); a bare "redirects=url" value is not supported.
# entrystore.auth.permitted.redirects.1=https://app.yourdomain.tld/
# entrystore.auth.permitted.redirects.2=https://portal.yourdomain.tld/

## HTTP Basic authentication (default: false)
## Renamed in 6.0 — previously `entrystore.auth.http-basic`. The legacy key is still
## accepted: on/true enable it as in 5.x, and a WARN names the new key. Check the
## startup log for "Basic Auth Enabled" / "Basic Auth Disabled" to verify the state.
## entrystore.auth.password=off disables HTTP Basic whatever this key says.
# entrystore.auth.http-basic.enabled=true|false

## HTTP Basic credential cache — successful PBKDF2 verifications are memoised
## so a repeated `Authorization: Basic …` from the same caller skips PBKDF2 for
## up to the TTL. Only takes effect when http-basic is enabled. A password
## change invalidates the entry immediately (new PBKDF2 salt → new encoded →
## new cache key) and disabled accounts are re-checked every request, so
## neither path waits for TTL. TTL only bounds forced credential invalidation
## — e.g., revoking a still-valid (raw, encoded) pair after a leak — which
## takes effect after at most TTL. Lower the TTL if you need post-incident
## revocation to propagate faster. max-size caps memory at one entry per
## (encoded-hash × raw-password) in flight; password rotation produces a new
## encoded → new key, so old and new entries coexist until TTL eviction
## (size for the rotation window, not just steady state).
# entrystore.auth.http-basic.cache.ttl=1h
# entrystore.auth.http-basic.cache.max-size=10000

## SAML authentication (default: false)
## Renamed in 6.0 — previously `entrystore.auth.saml` (on/new). Check the startup log for
## "SAML Auth Enabled" / "SAML Auth Disabled" to verify the state.
# entrystore.auth.saml.enabled=true|false
## Each IdP is a Spring Security relying-party registration, keyed by IdP id:
# spring.security.saml2.relyingparty.registration.myIdp.assertingparty.metadata-uri=https://idp.example.com/metadata
## SP entity id (default: {baseUrl}/saml2/service-provider-metadata/{id}); set it to keep the id the IdP knows.
# spring.security.saml2.relyingparty.registration.myIdp.entity-id=https://store.example.com/sp
## An IdP whose http(s) metadata cannot be fetched at startup (10 s connect / 30 s read timeout) does not stop
## EntryStore from starting: a WARN names it, and its metadata is retried in the background (every 5 min, or
## max-age if shorter) and on login (at most every 30 s); SAML login with it fails until then. A missing or
## invalid file: metadata still fails startup.
## The IdP posts its response to the assertion consumer service, set by
## spring.security.saml2.relyingparty.registration.{id}.acs.location (default: {baseUrl}/login/saml2/sso/{id}).
## /auth/saml?idp={id}, the 5.x URL, is also accepted, so an IdP set up for 5.x needs no change.
## Responses of up to 512 KB are accepted there; server.jetty.max-http-form-post-size (32KB) applies to other forms.
## Binding of the authentication request: post or redirect (default: the IdP metadata's first endpoint).
## The request goes to the metadata's endpoint for that binding, unless ...singlesignon.url is set.
# spring.security.saml2.relyingparty.registration.myIdp.assertingparty.singlesignon.binding=post
## 5.x SAML settings are still accepted: at startup they are translated to the keys above, and a
## WARN names each 6.x key to set instead. An explicitly set 6.x key wins over a translation.
## Any ...registration.{id}.* key stops the translation of IdP {id}: set all its 6.x keys, metadata-uri included.
##   entrystore.auth.saml=on|new                           -> entrystore.auth.saml.enabled
##   (idp.{id}.)relying-party-id                           -> ...registration.{id}.entity-id
##   (idp.{id}.)metadata.url / idp-metadata.url            -> ...registration.{id}.assertingparty.metadata-uri
##   (idp.{id}.)redirect-method=get|post (unset: get)      -> ...registration.{id}.assertingparty.singlesignon.binding=redirect|post
##   assertion-consumer-service.url (+ ?idp={id})          -> ...registration.{id}.acs.location
##   idp-metadata.max-age / user-auto-provisioning         -> entrystore.auth.saml.idp.{id}.metadata.max-age / .user-auto-provisioning
##   redirect-domain-whitelist.1, .2, ... (same for idp.{id}.domains) -> a comma-separated value
## No WARN names these, which a translated IdP also gets; set them when moving to the 6.x keys:
##   ...registration.{id}.name-id-format=urn:oasis:names:tc:SAML:1.1:nameid-format:unspecified
##   ...registration.{id}.assertingparty.singlesignon.binding=redirect (when redirect-method is unset)
##   ...registration.{id}.assertingparty.singlesignon.sign-request=false
## The 5.x single-IdP settings (entrystore.auth.saml=on) become the IdP id of default-idp, else "default".
## Default IdP id used when the request carries no idp/username hint.
# entrystore.auth.saml.default-idp=myIdp
## Per-IdP EntryStore settings, keyed by the same IdP id.
## domains: comma-separated email domains routed to this IdP (default: * — wildcard fallback).
## user-auto-provisioning: create the EntryStore user on first login (default: false).
## metadata.max-age: seconds before this IdP's asserting-party metadata is re-fetched at runtime, so
## signing-certificate rollovers are picked up without a restart (default: 604800 = 7 days, minimum 60).
# entrystore.auth.saml.idp.myIdp.domains=example.com,example.org
# entrystore.auth.saml.idp.myIdp.user-auto-provisioning=true
# entrystore.auth.saml.idp.myIdp.metadata.max-age=86400
## Comma-separated host whitelist for post-login successurl/failureurl redirects;
## a redirect to any host not listed here is dropped (open-redirect protection).
# entrystore.auth.saml.redirect-domain-whitelist=app.example.com,portal.example.com
## How long a started login may stay at the IdP (MFA, consent, password change) before its response is rejected
## and its successurl/failureurl are forgotten (default: 15m, 1m to 60m).
# entrystore.auth.saml.request-lifetime=15m

## CAS authentication (default: false)
## Renamed in 6.0 — previously `entrystore.auth.cas`. The legacy key is still accepted:
## on enables it as in 5.x, and a WARN names the new key. Check the startup log for
## "CAS Auth Enabled" / "CAS Auth Disabled" to verify the state.
# entrystore.auth.cas.enabled=true|false
# entrystore.auth.cas.server.url=https://cas.example.com/cas
## CAS login URL (default: {server.url}/login). Renamed in 6.0 — previously
## `entrystore.auth.cas.server.url.login`, which is still accepted with a WARN.
# entrystore.auth.cas.server.url-login=https://cas.example.com/cas/login

## OpenID Connect (OIDC) authentication (default: false)
## Check the startup log for "OIDC Auth Enabled" / "OIDC Auth Disabled" to verify the state.
# entrystore.auth.oidc.enabled=true|false
## Default provider id used when the request carries no provider/username hint.
# entrystore.auth.oidc.default-provider=myProvider
## Per-provider settings, keyed by provider id. The id must match the Spring client-registration id
## (spring.security.oauth2.client.registration.{id}.*). A provider is defined by the presence of any
## `provider.{id}.*` key.
## domains: comma-separated email domains routed to this provider (default: * — wildcard fallback).
## user-auto-provisioning: create the EntryStore user on first login (default: false).
## username-claim: claim mapped to the EntryStore username, resolved from the merged ID-token and
## userinfo claims (default: email). The registration's scopes must make the provider emit the
## claim (e.g. scope=openid,profile,email).
# entrystore.auth.oidc.provider.myProvider.domains=example.com,example.org
# entrystore.auth.oidc.provider.myProvider.user-auto-provisioning=true
# entrystore.auth.oidc.provider.myProvider.username-claim=email
## Comma-separated host whitelist for post-login successurl/failureurl redirects;
## a redirect to any host not listed here is dropped (open-redirect protection).
# entrystore.auth.oidc.redirect-domain-whitelist=app.example.com,portal.example.com
## Post-login redirect targets when the request carries no (whitelisted) successurl/failureurl
## (default: /auth/user for both).
# entrystore.auth.oidc.redirect-success.url=https://app.example.com/
# entrystore.auth.oidc.redirect-failure.url=https://app.example.com/login
## Client id/secret, scopes and issuer are configured via Spring Boot's native properties. The
## issuer-uri drives OIDC discovery (.well-known/openid-configuration) including JWKS key rotation.
# spring.security.oauth2.client.registration.myProvider.client-id=entrystore
# spring.security.oauth2.client.registration.myProvider.client-secret=...
# spring.security.oauth2.client.registration.myProvider.scope=openid,profile,email
# spring.security.oauth2.client.provider.myProvider.issuer-uri=https://idp.example.com/realms/myrealm

## Solr
## Maximum number of rows a search or a syndication feed returns.
## When unset the two consumers differ: search caps at 100, syndication at 50.
## Setting this applies one cap to both.
# entrystore.solr.max-limit=200
entrystore.solr=on
